WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.576
A release job has to write to a registry, so it needs a key.

2
00:00:06.576 --> 00:00:09.482
The simple way is to store a key as a secret.

3
00:00:09.482 --> 00:00:14.197
That key lives for months, works from anywhere, and is painful to rotate.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.239
If it leaks, an attacker can use it until someone notices.

5
00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:20.621
Here is how release.yml does better.

6
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:25.713
It starts when you push a version tag, such as v1.2.0.

7
00:00:25.713 --> 00:00:30.151
Any tag that matches v star starts a run of release.yml.

8
00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:36.342
Without a gate, every tag you push ships at once, even one you pushed by mistake.

9
00:00:36.342 --> 00:00:39.208
So the job names an environment: production.

10
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:43.466
Before the job starts, GitHub checks the environment's rules.

11
00:00:43.466 --> 00:00:45.185
Here, a required reviewer.

12
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.979
The run stops and waits.

13
00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:50.533
It can wait up to thirty days for a person to say yes.

14
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:53.227
One approval lets the job go on.

15
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:56.085
Now the job starts, on a fresh machine.

16
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.039
Every job gets a token without asking: the GITHUB_TOKEN.

17
00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:04.847
GitHub creates it when the job starts, and it expires when the job ends.

18
00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:07.648
It only works on the current repository.

19
00:01:07.648 --> 00:01:09.261
But what may it do there?

20
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.948
You decide, with the permissions key.

21
00:01:11.948 --> 00:01:16.776
At the top of the workflow, the default for every job is contents: read.

22
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.509
This job alone adds packages: write, to push images.

23
00:01:20.509 --> 00:01:23.477
Any permission you do not list becomes none.

24
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.387
That is least privilege.

25
00:01:25.387 --> 00:01:28.620
The token gets only the access its task needs.

26
00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:30.889
Then the steps run in order.

27
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.293
Checkout fetches the code at the tag.

28
00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:39.817
QEMU, an emulator, lets this amd64 runner build arm64 steps too.

29
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:45.587
The login step logs in to ghcr.io with the job's own GITHUB_TOKEN.

30
00:01:45.587 --> 00:01:48.196
No password is stored anywhere.

31
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:56.300
The metadata step turns the git tag into image tags: 1.2.0, sha plus the short commit, and latest.

32
00:01:56.533 --> 00:02:03.495
Then build and push makes the image for both CPU types, and pushes them under one multi-platform tag.

33
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:04.877
Here is the catch.

34
00:02:04.877 --> 00:02:09.585
Every action in the job can use the token, even if you never pass it on.

35
00:02:09.585 --> 00:02:12.659
Whatever the token may do, every action may do.

36
00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:17.797
And an action is someone else's code, running with your token.

37
00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:21.004
A tag such as v4 is only a label on one commit.

38
00:02:21.233 --> 00:02:25.168
Whoever controls the action's repository can move it.

39
00:02:25.168 --> 00:02:35.412
In March 2025 that really happened: the tags of a popular action were moved to a malicious commit, which printed CI secrets into the logs.

40
00:02:35.633 --> 00:02:37.968
A full commit SHA cannot be moved.

41
00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:43.666
So release.yml pins every third-party action to one, with the version in a comment.

42
00:02:43.666 --> 00:02:46.000
Dependabot moves the pins for you.

43
00:02:46.233 --> 00:02:48.993
When the job ends, the token expires.

44
00:02:48.993 --> 00:02:53.393
What is left is the image in GHCR, built once from the tag.

45
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.449
A tag starts the run.

46
00:02:55.449 --> 00:02:57.105
A person opens the gate.

47
00:02:57.105 --> 00:03:04.559
The job gets a short-lived token with only the rights it needs, and pinned actions use it to push one image.
